1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,035
Lesson 5.1.

2
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:06,450
Why digital hardware is
inefficient for probabilistic AI.

3
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:11,466
A GPU is an extraordinary
machine for multiplying matrices.

4
00:00:11,846 --> 00:00:16,812
But sampling from a probabilistic model
is something else: many small updates,

5
00:00:16,812 --> 00:00:21,333
each with its own random number, that
read their neighbors from memory.

6
00:00:21,713 --> 00:00:26,945
In this lesson we take apart the cost of
one Gibbs update on digital hardware and

7
00:00:26,945 --> 00:00:31,531
see why a noisy physical device can
make it thousands of times cheaper.

8
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:37,330
Updating a spin with sixteen neighbors
requires reading sixteen states

9
00:00:37,330 --> 00:00:41,943
and sixteen couplings, summing the
local field, evaluating a sigmoid,

10
00:00:41,943 --> 00:00:46,021
generating a random number,
comparing and writing the result.

11
00:00:46,401 --> 00:00:49,421
The arithmetic is minimal:
about thirty operations.

12
00:00:49,801 --> 00:00:54,171
But every datum travels from
memory, and in the table from lesson

13
00:00:54,171 --> 00:00:58,608
0.1, reading a word from on-chip
memory already costs more than an

14
00:00:58,608 --> 00:01:02,575
addition, and from external
memory, hundreds of times more.

15
00:01:03,683 --> 00:01:06,739
In the roofline model,
the Gibbs update performs

16
00:01:06,739 --> 00:01:09,539
on the order of one
operation per byte read.

17
00:01:09,919 --> 00:01:13,094
It sits far to the left
of the knee: memory-bound.

18
00:01:13,474 --> 00:01:16,612
The GPU spends most of
its time waiting for

19
00:01:16,612 --> 00:01:19,312
data, with its arithmetic units idle.

20
00:01:19,692 --> 00:01:23,714
And the sparse graph of an
Ising model does not lend itself

21
00:01:23,714 --> 00:01:27,122
to cache reuse like a dense
matrix multiplication.

22
00:01:28,250 --> 00:01:31,364
In addition, every update
needs a random number.

23
00:01:31,744 --> 00:01:35,719
A digital generator, like
Philox or xorshift, manufactures

24
00:01:35,719 --> 00:01:40,311
pseudorandom bits with several
multiplications and shifts per word.

25
00:01:40,691 --> 00:01:43,149
It is determinism
disguised as chance, and it

26
00:01:43,149 --> 00:01:45,280
is paid for with operations and energy.

27
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:49,577
A physical device, instead,
already fluctuates: according to

28
00:01:49,577 --> 00:01:53,560
the syllabus, an all-transistor
generator based on shot noise

29
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:56,759
produces a random bit for
hundreds of attojoules.

30
00:01:57,883 --> 00:02:01,227
The third cost is
invisible: noise margins.

31
00:02:01,607 --> 00:02:05,508
Each digital bit is stored with
barriers of tens of k_B T so

32
00:02:05,508 --> 00:02:09,214
that thermal noise never flips
it, as we saw in unit one.

33
00:02:09,594 --> 00:02:13,840
For a sampler that is absurd: we
spend energy removing noise and then

34
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:17,963
more energy manufacturing it again
with the pseudorandom generator.

35
00:02:18,343 --> 00:02:23,040
A pbit works with barriers on the order
of k_B T and uses the noise directly.

36
00:02:24,150 --> 00:02:26,514
Let's make an order-of-magnitude estimate.

37
00:02:26,894 --> 00:02:30,700
If an update reads about twelve
words from on-chip memory at

38
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:34,570
five picojoules each, plus a
few picojoules of arithmetic and

39
00:02:34,570 --> 00:02:38,122
randomness, it costs on the
order of seventy picojoules.

40
00:02:38,502 --> 00:02:43,779
The Z1 chip states under one watt for
two hundred sixty-nine thousand pbits

41
00:02:43,779 --> 00:02:48,282
at fifty megahertz: at most
seventy-four femtojoules per update.

42
00:02:48,662 --> 00:02:50,867
The difference is about
a thousand times, and

43
00:02:50,867 --> 00:02:52,876
that is without counting external memory.

44
00:02:53,983 --> 00:02:57,975
The design lesson is clear:
compute where the data lives,

45
00:02:58,355 --> 00:03:02,764
storing the couplings next to each
pbit; use physical noise instead of

46
00:03:02,764 --> 00:03:07,047
pseudorandom generators; and read
out results as little as possible,

47
00:03:07,427 --> 00:03:11,464
because input, output and
digital glue can dominate

48
00:03:11,464 --> 00:03:15,263
the system budget, as we
will see in lesson 5.8.

49
00:03:16,383 --> 00:03:20,477
Frequent mistakes: comparing
operations instead of bytes moved.

50
00:03:20,857 --> 00:03:25,373
Comparing a chip's fabric energy
with a GPU's system energy.

51
00:03:25,753 --> 00:03:28,505
Ignoring the cost of the
pseudorandom generator.

52
00:03:28,885 --> 00:03:32,609
And treating order-of-magnitude
estimates as if they were measurements.

53
00:03:33,717 --> 00:03:38,628
In summary: on digital hardware, a
Gibbs update pays for data movement,

54
00:03:38,628 --> 00:03:43,817
randomness generation and noise margins,
and the arithmetic is almost free.

55
00:03:44,197 --> 00:03:46,449
Sampling is memory-bound.

56
00:03:46,829 --> 00:03:51,155
A physical pbit, with its couplings
next to it and noise as a resource,

57
00:03:51,155 --> 00:03:55,177
can be on the order of a thousand
times more efficient per update.

58
00:03:56,283 --> 00:04:00,520
To practice: estimate the
arithmetic intensity of an update.

59
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:04,938
Bound the Z1 energy per update
and compare it with Landauer.

60
00:04:05,318 --> 00:04:07,658
Explain why the GPU sits idle.

61
00:04:08,038 --> 00:04:10,864
And list the three costs a pbit avoids.
